            </div>
            <div class="tw-bars">
              <div class="tw-row">
                <span class="tw-lbl">bytes / 8 elem</span>
                <span class="tw-track"><span class="tw-bar tw-bytes" id="mpk-bytes-bar" style="width:25%"></span></span>
                <span class="tw-val" id="mpk-bytes-val">4 B</span>
              </div>
              <div class="tw-row">
                <span class="tw-lbl">instr / 8 elem</span>
                <span class="tw-track"><span class="tw-bar tw-instr" id="mpk-instr-bar" style="width:31%"></span></span>
                <span class="tw-val" id="mpk-instr-val">≈ 10–12</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="cross-verdict" id="mpk-cross-verdict">
              <span class="lang-en">INT4, fused: 4× fewer bytes for ~10–12 instructions per 8 elements — stays memory-bound; the savings are real (KVQuant: 1.2–1.7× faster than fp16 at batch 1).</span>
              <span class="lang-ko">INT4, fused: byte 4× 감소에 8 element당 instruction ~10–12개 — memory-bound가 유지되고 절약은 진짜다 (KVQuant: batch 1에서 fp16보다 1.2–1.7× 빠름).</span>
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- right: batch decay curve -->
          <div class="viz-frame cross-card">
            <h3 class="cross-h"><span class="lang-en">speedup vs batch — MARLIN’s measured decay (A10, 4-bit weights)</span><span class="lang-ko">speedup vs batch — MARLIN의 실측 감쇠 (A10, 4-bit weight)</span></h3>
            <div class="mpk-scroll">
              <svg class="mpk-svg" viewBox="0 0 760 300" style="min-width: 560px" role="img"
                   aria-label="Speedup versus batch size: about 3.9 times up to batch 32, decaying to 1.5 times at batch 128, with the memory-to-compute crossover shaded near batch 50 to 64">
                {[1, 2, 3, 4].map((s) => (
                  <g>
                    <line x1="60" y1={cY(s)} x2="700" y2={cY(s)} stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"></line>
                    <text x="50" y={cY(s) + 4} text-anchor="end" class="t-tiny t-tert">{s}×</text>
                  </g>
                ))}
                <rect x={cX(50)} y="40" width={cX(64) - cX(50)} height="220" fill="var(--accent-soft)" opacity="0.55"
                      data-tip="A10 FLOPs/byte ≈ 200 → memory-bound below batch ≈ 50–64, compute-bound above (MARLIN roofline). A100-40GB has nearly the same FP16-TC ratio (≈200) — the transfer is an estimate."
                      data-tip-ko="A10 FLOPs/byte ≈ 200 → batch ≈ 50–64 아래는 memory-bound, 위는 compute-bound (MARLIN roofline). A100-40GB도 FP16-TC 비율이 거의 같아서 (≈200) 이 전이는 추정으로 성립한다."></rect>
                <text x={cX(57)} y="34" text-anchor="middle" class="t-tiny t-accent">crossover b ≈ 50–64</text>
                <polyline points={curvePts} fill="none" stroke="var(--accent-strong)" stroke-width="2.4"></polyline>
                {marlinPts.map(([b, s]) => (
                  <circle cx={cX(b)} cy={cY(s)} r="3.5" fill="var(--accent-strong)"
                          data-tip={`batch ${b}: ≈ ${s}× (sketch through MARLIN’s published points)`}
                          data-tip-ko={`batch ${b}: ≈ ${s}× (MARLIN 공개 수치를 잇는 스케치)`}></circle>
                ))}
                <circle id="mpk-batch-dot" cx={cX(32)} cy={cY(3.85)} r="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--accent-strong)" stroke-width="2.5"></circle>
                {[16, 32, 64, 96, 128].map((b) => (
                  <text x={cX(b)} y="280" text-anchor="middle" class="t-tiny t-tert">{b}</text>
                ))}
                <text x="380" y="298" text-anchor="middle" class="t-tiny t-tert">batch size</text>
                <text x="60" y="26" class="t-tiny t-tert">kernel speedup over FP16 GEMM (curve sketched through MARLIN’s reported values — labeled points)</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="slider-row">
              <span class="slider-lbl">batch</span>
              <input type="range" id="mpk-batch-slider" min="1" max="128" step="1" value="32" aria-label="Batch size" />
              <span class="slider-val" id="mpk-batch-val">b = 32 · ≈ 3.9×</span>
            </div>
            <p class="cross-verdict" id="mpk-batch-verdict">
              <span class="lang-en">Memory-bound regime: MARLIN holds close to the maximum 4× for batches up to 16–32; FLUTE’s LUT gains likewise concentrate below batch 32.</span>
              <span class="lang-ko">Memory-bound regime: MARLIN은 batch 16–32까지 최대 4×에 근접하게 유지한다; FLUTE의 LUT 이득도 batch 32 아래에 몰린다.</span>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="mpk-note">
          <span class="lang-en">
            The honest summary, regime by regime: at batch 1 with fused kernels the naive
            byte-counting story largely holds — KVQuant’s low-bit KV kernels beat fp16 outright.
            Naive per-element dequant loses even there (QServe: 4-bit KV 1.1–1.2× slower than
            8-bit on A100). And as batch grows the baseline roofline rises to meet you: MARLIN’s
            fully-engineered kernel decays from ~3.9× to ~1.5× by batch 128 (end-to-end in vLLM:
            2.8× at batch 16 → ~1.2× at 128). Every added op per byte moves that crossover
            earlier.
          </span><span class="lang-ko">
            regime별 정직한 요약: batch 1 + fused kernel에서는 byte 계산 그대로의 이야기가 대체로
            성립한다 — KVQuant의 low-bit KV kernel은 fp16을 완전히 이긴다. naive element별
            dequant는 거기서도 진다 (QServe: A100에서 4-bit KV가 8-bit보다 1.1–1.2× 느림). 그리고
            batch가 커지면 baseline의 roofline이 올라와 마중 나온다: 완전히 엔지니어링된 MARLIN
            kernel도 batch 128에서는 ~3.9× → ~1.5×로 감쇠한다 (vLLM end-to-end: batch 16에서
            2.8× → 128에서 ~1.2×). byte당 op가 하나 늘 때마다 그 crossover는 앞당겨진다.
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ prose ============ -->
    <section class="section-tight">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2><span class="lang-en">Reading the diagram</span><span class="lang-ko">다이어그램 읽는 법</span></h2>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              The through-line of this page is a single accounting identity: a decode-attention
              kernel is fast when its time equals bytes ÷ bandwidth, and every instruction spent
              on unpack, dequant, addressing, or gather is a claim against that identity. All
              hardware numbers are the A100’s (GA100 whitepaper, CUDA docs); all kernel numbers
              are the cited papers’; derived arithmetic is labeled as an estimate.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              이 페이지의 관통선은 하나의 회계 항등식이다: decode-attention kernel은 시간이
              bytes ÷ bandwidth와 같을 때 빠르고, unpack·dequant·주소 계산·gather에 쓰는 모든
              instruction은 그 항등식에 대한 청구다. 하드웨어 숫자는 전부 A100의 것(GA100
              whitepaper, CUDA docs)이고, kernel 숫자는 인용된 논문의 것이며, 유도된 산술은
              추정으로 표시했다.
            </span>
          </p>

          <h3>a — <span class="lang-en">Promise vs path</span><span class="lang-ko">약속 vs 경로</span></h3>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              KV reads at decode time have arithmetic intensity ~1 MAC/element regardless of batch
              (QServe), so they are DRAM-bound and byte savings <em>should</em> convert to time —
              KVQuant states this as the motivation for accepting “a more complex dequantization
              process.” The catch is that the added complexity executes on CUDA cores, whose
              roofline is ≈ 9.8 ops per byte on A100-80GB (QServe; ≈ 12.5 on the 40 GB part,
              estimate) — about 1/50th of the tensor-core budget per QServe’s comparison. The path
              diagram exists to make the two added stages — UNPACK and DEQUANT — visible as
              first-class citizens of the dataflow.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              decode 시점의 KV 읽기는 batch와 무관하게 element당 ~1 MAC의 arithmetic
              intensity라서 (QServe) DRAM-bound이고, byte 절약은 시간으로 환전<em>되어야</em>
              한다 — KVQuant는 이것을 “더 복잡한 dequantization”을 감수하는 동기로 명시한다.
              함정은 그 추가 복잡성이 CUDA core에서 실행된다는 것이고, 그 roofline은 A100-80GB
              기준 byte당 ≈ 9.8 ops다 (QServe; 40 GB 모델은 ≈ 12.5, 추정) — QServe의 비교로는
              tensor-core 예산의 약 1/50. path 다이어그램은 추가된 두 stage — UNPACK과 DEQUANT
              — 를 dataflow의 일급 시민으로 보이게 하려고 있다.
            </span>
          </p>

          <h3>b — <span class="lang-en">The walkthrough and the meter</span><span class="lang-ko">walkthrough와 meter</span></h3>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              The per-8-element instruction counts are estimates anchored at both ends to QServe’s
              published figures: 5 ALU ops per element for naive dequant (≈ 32–40 per 8) and ~2
              per element optimized (≈ 10–12 per 8, matching the lop3/prmt sequence MARLIN
              documents). Divide by bytes moved and you get the meter: naive INT4 lands at ~10
              ops/byte — level with the 9.8 budget, which is why QServe measured naive 4-bit KV
              running 1.1–1.2× <em>slower</em> than 8-bit on A100. INT2 halves the bytes without
              shrinking the per-element work, doubling ops/byte: the arithmetic reason 2-bit is
              not 2× faster than 4-bit without heroic kernels.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              8 element당 instruction 수는 양 끝을 QServe의 공개 수치에 앵커한 추정이다: naive
              dequant는 element당 5 ALU ops (8개당 ≈ 32–40), 최적화하면 ~2 (8개당 ≈ 10–12,
              MARLIN이 기록한 lop3/prmt 시퀀스와 일치). 이동한 byte로 나누면 meter가 된다:
              naive INT4는 ~10 ops/byte — 예산 9.8과 같은 높이이고, 그래서 QServe는 A100에서
              naive 4-bit KV가 8-bit보다 1.1–1.2× <em>느린</em> 것을 실측했다. INT2는
              element당 작업을 줄이지 못한 채 byte만 반으로 줄여 ops/byte를 2배로 만든다:
              heroic kernel 없이는 2-bit가 4-bit보다 2× 빠르지 않은 산술적 이유다.
            </span>
          </p>

          <h3>c — <span class="lang-en">The second stream</span><span class="lang-ko">두 번째 stream</span></h3>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              Scales and zeros are real traffic: at KIVI’s group size 32, a “2-bit” cache stores 3
              effective bits — metadata is half the payload — and the FP16 residual window (R=128
              newest tokens) lifts a 512-token context to ≥ 6.25 bits/element (estimates from
              verified settings). Mixed precision adds a third stream: width tags are cheap in
              bytes but must be branched on, and variable-length records force either runtime
              prefix sums or stored offsets — a dependent load in front of every payload load.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              scale과 zero는 실제 트래픽이다: KIVI의 group size 32에서 “2-bit” cache는 3
              effective bits를 저장하고 — metadata가 payload의 절반 — FP16 residual window(최신
              R=128 token)는 512-token context를 ≥ 6.25 bits/element로 끌어올린다 (검증된 설정
              위의 추정). mixed precision은 세 번째 stream을 더한다: width tag는 byte로는 싸지만
              분기 대상이고, 가변 길이 record는 runtime prefix sum 아니면 저장된 offset을
              강제한다 — 모든 payload load 앞의 dependent load다.
            </span>
          </p>

          <h3>d — <span class="lang-en">Why irregularity, specifically, is expensive</span><span class="lang-ko">왜 하필 불규칙성이 비싼가</span></h3>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              The three failures are all the same fact seen from different units: the GPU’s cheap
              paths — affine addresses, one instruction per warp, 32-byte sectors consumed whole —
              assume uniformity. Data-dependent widths spend CUDA-core cycles on pointer
              arithmetic (QServe: 32× below tensor-core throughput), serialize divergent lanes
              (the SIMT rule: each branch path executes with other lanes disabled), and waste
              fetched sectors. The 0-bit class turns reads into gathers — and SqueezeLLM’s
              measurement (0.45% sparse ⇒ ~10% latency) is the cleanest published price tag for
              that bookkeeping.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              세 failure는 같은 사실을 다른 단위에서 본 것이다: GPU의 싼 경로 — affine 주소,
              warp당 한 instruction, 통째로 소비되는 32-byte sector — 는 균일성을 가정한다.
              데이터 의존적 width는 CUDA-core cycle을 pointer 산술에 쓰게 하고 (QServe:
              tensor-core throughput의 1/32), divergent lane을 직렬화하며 (SIMT 규칙: 각 branch
              경로가 나머지 lane을 끈 채 실행된다), fetch한 sector를 낭비한다. 0-bit class는
              읽기를 gather로 바꾼다 — SqueezeLLM의 실측(0.45% sparse ⇒ latency ~10%)이 그
              장부 관리의 가장 깔끔한 공개 가격표다.
            </span>
          </p>

          <h3>e·f — <span class="lang-en">The fixes, and the regime they buy</span><span class="lang-ko">fix들, 그리고 그것이 사는 regime</span></h3>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              Every fix restores a uniformity the hardware wants: pools make each warp’s tile
              single-width (Atom → DiffKV), bitplanes make width a read-count instead of a branch
              (Any-Precision), offline permutation moves shuffles to pack time (MARLIN, QServe,
              FLUTE), LUT replication de-serializes shared memory (FLUTE), and pipelining hides
              the remaining instructions under DRAM time — until registers run out. What none of
              them change is the regime boundary: MARLIN’s own curve decays from ~3.9× to ~1.5×
              by batch 128, because past the roofline crossover the baseline is no longer waiting
              on the bytes you saved.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              모든 fix는 하드웨어가 원하는 균일성을 복원한다: pool은 warp의 tile을 단일 width로
              만들고 (Atom → DiffKV), bitplane은 width를 분기가 아닌 읽기 횟수로 바꾸고
              (Any-Precision), offline permutation은 shuffle을 pack 시점으로 옮기고 (MARLIN,
              QServe, FLUTE), LUT 복제는 shared memory의 직렬화를 풀고 (FLUTE), pipelining은
              남은 instruction을 DRAM 시간 밑에 숨긴다 — register가 바닥나기 전까지. 어느 것도
              바꾸지 못하는 것은 regime의 경계다: MARLIN 자신의 곡선도 batch 128에서 ~3.9× →
              ~1.5×로 감쇠한다. roofline crossover를 지나면 baseline은 더 이상 당신이 아낀
              byte를 기다리고 있지 않기 때문이다.
            </span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ why it matters ============ -->
    <section class="section-tight">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="why-band band-dark">
          <h3><span class="lang-en">Why this matters</span><span class="lang-ko">왜 중요한가</span></h3>
          <p>
            <span class="lang-en">
              “Bytes ÷ bandwidth” is the most seductive model in inference acceleration, and it is
              right just often enough to be dangerous. The published record is unambiguous on both
              sides: fused low-bit KV kernels genuinely beat fp16 at batch 1 (KVQuant), and a
              naive 4-bit KV kernel genuinely lost to 8-bit on the same silicon (QServe) — the
              difference is nothing but the instruction stream between the load and the FMA.
              Reading compression papers with this page’s meter in mind turns “N× smaller” claims
              into checkable questions: how many ops per byte, on which pipe, against which
              baseline, at what batch.
            </span><span class="lang-ko">
              “bytes ÷ bandwidth”는 inference 가속에서 가장 유혹적인 모델이고, 위험할 만큼만
              자주 맞는다. 공개된 기록은 양쪽 모두 명확하다: fused low-bit KV kernel은 batch
              1에서 fp16을 실제로 이기고 (KVQuant), naive 4-bit KV kernel은 같은 실리콘에서
              8-bit에 실제로 졌다 (QServe) — 차이는 load와 FMA 사이의 instruction stream뿐이다.
              이 페이지의 meter를 염두에 두고 압축 논문을 읽으면 “N× 작다”는 주장이 검증
              가능한 질문으로 바뀐다: byte당 몇 ops를, 어느 pipe에서, 어떤 baseline 상대로,
              어떤 batch에서.
            </span>
          </p>
          <div class="why-chips">
            <span class="why-chip">~1 MAC/element, any batch (QServe)</span>
            <span class="why-chip">CUDA-core budget ≈ 9.8–12.5 ops/B</span>
            <span class="why-chip">naive INT4 = 5 ops/elem → 1.1–1.2× slower</span>
            <span class="why-chip">fused KV4 @ b1 = 1.2–1.7× faster (KVQuant)</span>
            <span class="why-chip">MARLIN: 3.9× @ b16–32 → 1.5× @ b128</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

